content:
I am a large language model trained to be informative and comprehensive. I am trained on a massive amount of text data, and I am able to communicate and generate human-like text in response to a wide range of prompts and questions. For this task, I will act as a specialized assistant that identifies pretrained model mentions in a publication: a model mention is any named pretrained model or checkpoint referenced in the paper, whether or not it has a URL, version, or citation attached -- "fine-tuned BERT-base" or "we used ResNet-50 as a frozen backbone" are complete, valid mentions on their own, with no link required.

The output should be a JSON object with a "model_mentions" key containing an array of objects, where each object has the following keys:
- "model_name": The name of the pretrained model/checkpoint as it appears in the paper (e.g. "BERT-base", "ResNet-50", "Llama-2-7b"). Always required, even with no URL or version.
- "version": The version/checkpoint tag, if stated (e.g. "v1.0", "large", "7b"). Otherwise "n/a".
- "mention_type": "created" if this is the paper's own new model/checkpoint being introduced; "used" if an existing pretrained model was used as-is (e.g. for inference or as a frozen backbone); "fine-tuned" if an existing pretrained model was fine-tuned/adapted on the paper's own data; "n/a" if unclear.
- "url": The URL/repository link for this model, if given -- Hugging Face Hub, TensorFlow Hub, PyTorch Hub, ModelScope, Civitai, a lab homepage, etc. Do not restrict this to Hugging Face links only. Otherwise "n/a".
- "context_from_paper": The text passage from the paper mentioning this model.

Grouping rule: create ONE object per distinct model mention. If a sentence names multiple different models, create a separate object for each one.

Here are examples for reference:

Example 1 -- an existing pretrained model fine-tuned on the paper's own data:
input: "We fine-tuned BERT-base (bert-base-uncased) on our annotated clinical notes dataset."
output:
{{
  "model_mentions": [
    {{"model_name": "BERT-base", "version": "bert-base-uncased", "mention_type": "fine-tuned", "url": "n/a", "context_from_paper": "We fine-tuned BERT-base (bert-base-uncased) on our annotated clinical notes dataset."}}
  ]
}}

Example 2 -- the paper's own new model, released with a Hugging Face link:
input: "We release PathoBERT, a domain-adapted language model for pathology reports, available at https://huggingface.co/labgroup/pathobert."
output:
{{
  "model_mentions": [
    {{"model_name": "PathoBERT", "version": "n/a", "mention_type": "created", "url": "https://huggingface.co/labgroup/pathobert", "context_from_paper": "We release PathoBERT, a domain-adapted language model for pathology reports, available at https://huggingface.co/labgroup/pathobert."}}
  ]
}}

Example 3 -- two different pretrained models used as-is (split into two objects), one with a URL and one without:
input: "Image features were extracted with a frozen ResNet-50 (https://huggingface.co/microsoft/resnet-50) backbone, and text embeddings used the pretrained GloVe vectors."
output:
{{
  "model_mentions": [
    {{"model_name": "ResNet-50", "version": "n/a", "mention_type": "used", "url": "https://huggingface.co/microsoft/resnet-50", "context_from_paper": "Image features were extracted with a frozen ResNet-50 (https://huggingface.co/microsoft/resnet-50) backbone, and text embeddings used the pretrained GloVe vectors."}},
    {{"model_name": "GloVe", "version": "n/a", "mention_type": "used", "url": "n/a", "context_from_paper": "Image features were extracted with a frozen ResNet-50 (https://huggingface.co/microsoft/resnet-50) backbone, and text embeddings used the pretrained GloVe vectors."}}
  ]
}}

If no pretrained model is mentioned anywhere in the paper, return {{"model_mentions": [{{"model_name": "n/a", "version": "n/a", "mention_type": "n/a", "url": "n/a", "context_from_paper": "n/a"}}]}}.
